from jsonb_to_recordset($gold$[{"id": "t1a-commute-b6.0", "task_type": "task1_academic", "prompt": "The table below shows how people in one city travelled to work in 2000 and 2020, as a percentage of all commuters. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.\n\nData (% of commuters, 2000 then 2020):\nCar: 45, 30\nBus: 25, 20\nWalking: 20, 15\nMetro: 5, 15\nBicycle: 5, 20", "text": "The table shows the ways people travelled to work in one city in 2000 and 2020.\n\nOverall, the car was the most popular way of travelling in both years, but it became less popular, while cycling and the metro became more popular.\n\nIn 2000, 45% of people used a car to go to work, which was almost half of all commuters. The bus was the second most common with 25%, and walking was 20%. Only 5% of people used the metro and the same percentage cycled.\n\nIn 2020 the situation was changed. The percentage of car users decreased to 30%, and the bus also fell slightly to 20%. On the other hand, the number of people cycling increased a lot, from 5% to 20%, so it became equal with the bus. The metro also grew from 5% to 15%. Walking decreased a little, from 20% to 15%.\n\nIn conclusion, people in this city used cars less and used bicycles and metro more in 2020 than in 2000.", "overall_band": 6.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 6, "coherence_cohesion": 6, "lexical_resource": 6, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 6}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1a-commute-b8.0", "task_type": "task1_academic", "prompt": "The table below shows how people in one city travelled to work in 2000 and 2020, as a percentage of all commuters. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.\n\nData (% of commuters, 2000 then 2020):\nCar: 45, 30\nBus: 25, 20\nWalking: 20, 15\nMetro: 5, 15\nBicycle: 5, 20", "text": "The table illustrates how commuters in a particular city travelled to work in 2000 and 2020.\n\nThe most striking change over the two decades was a shift away from the car towards cycling and the metro, although driving remained the single most common way of getting to work.\n\nIn 2000, almost half of all commuters (45%) drove, far more than those who took the bus (25%) or walked (20%). The metro and the bicycle were marginal options, each accounting for a mere 5% of journeys.\n\nBy 2020, the car's share had fallen by a third, to 30%. The biggest gains were made by cycling, which quadrupled to 20% and thus drew level with the bus, whose share had dipped slightly. The metro also tripled its share, rising to 15%. Walking also declined, falling from a fifth of commuters to 15%.\n\nTaken together, these figures suggest that commuting in the city became considerably more diverse, with active and public transport gaining ground at the expense of private cars.", "overall_band": 8.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 8, "coherence_cohesion": 8, "lexical_resource": 8, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 8}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1a-internet-b5.0", "task_type": "task1_academic", "prompt": "The table below shows the percentage of households with internet access in Kazakhstan, Poland and Brazil in 2005, 2010, 2015 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.\n\nData (households with internet access, %):\nKazakhstan: 2005 10, 2010 30, 2015 65, 2020 88\nPoland: 2005 30, 2010 55, 2015 75, 2020 90\nBrazil: 2005 15, 2010 35, 2015 50, 2020 70", "text": "The table show the percentage of households which have internet in Kazakhstan, Poland and Brazil from 2005 to 2020.\n\nIn Kazakhstan, in 2005 there was 10% of households with internet. In 2010 it was 30%. In 2015 it increase to 65% and in 2020 it was 88%.\n\nIn Poland, in 2005 it was 30%. In 2010 it was 55% and in 2015 it was 75%. In 2020 it was 90%, it is the highest.\n\nIn Brazil in 2005 the percentage was 15%. In 2010 it increased to 35%. In 2015 it was 50% and in 2020 it reach 75%.\n\nPoland was the higher in all years. Kazakhstan was lower than Brazil in 2005 but after that Kazakhstan was more high than Brazil. All countries increased.", "overall_band": 5.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 5, "coherence_cohesion": 5, "lexical_resource": 5, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 5}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1a-internet-b7.0", "task_type": "task1_academic", "prompt": "The table below shows the percentage of households with internet access in Kazakhstan, Poland and Brazil in 2005, 2010, 2015 and 2020. Summarise the information by selecting and reporting the main features, and make comparisons where relevant. Write at least 150 words.\n\nData (households with internet access, %):\nKazakhstan: 2005 10, 2010 30, 2015 65, 2020 88\nPoland: 2005 30, 2010 55, 2015 75, 2020 90\nBrazil: 2005 15, 2010 35, 2015 50, 2020 70", "text": "The table compares the proportion of households with internet access in Kazakhstan, Poland and Brazil at five-year intervals between 2005 and 2020.\n\nOverall, internet access rose substantially in all three countries. Poland had the highest rate throughout the period, while Kazakhstan saw the most dramatic growth, eventually overtaking Brazil by a wide margin.\n\nIn 2005, Poland was already well ahead, with 30% of households connected, compared with 15% in Brazil and only 10% in Kazakhstan. Over the next five years, all three figures rose sharply, reaching 55%, 35% and 30% respectively.\n\nThe period from 2010 to 2015 marked a turning point for Kazakhstan, where the figure more than doubled to 65%, pushing it ahead of Brazil, which reached just half of households. By 2020, Kazakhstan had almost caught up with Poland, at 88% and 90% respectively, whereas Brazil lagged behind at 70%.\n\nIn short, although Poland remained the leader, the gap between the three countries narrowed considerably, with Kazakhstan's growth being particularly rapid.", "overall_band": 7.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 7, "coherence_cohesion": 7, "lexical_resource": 7, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 7}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1g-laptop-b4.5", "task_type": "task1_general", "prompt": "You recently bought a laptop from an online shop, but when it arrived it was damaged. Write a letter to the shop. In your letter:\n- describe the laptop you bought\n- explain what the problem is\n- say what you would like the shop to do\nWrite at least 150 words. You do NOT need to write any addresses. Begin your letter as follows: Dear Sir or Madam,", "text": "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writting to you about the laptop what i buy from your shop last week. It is a laptop Lenovo, black color, and it cost 400 dollars. I buy it for my study because i am student.\n\nWhen the laptop come to my home, the box was broken and the screen of the laptop have a big crack. Also the laptop dont turn on when i press the button. I try to charge it but nothing happen. I was very angry because i need it for my exams and i pay a lot of money.\n\nI want that you give me new laptop or give back my money. Please answer me quickly because it is very important for me. You can call me on my phone.\n\nYours faithfully,\nAida", "overall_band": 4.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 5, "coherence_cohesion": 4, "lexical_resource": 5, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 4}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1g-laptop-b7.5", "task_type": "task1_general", "prompt": "You recently bought a laptop from an online shop, but when it arrived it was damaged. Write a letter to the shop. In your letter:\n- describe the laptop you bought\n- explain what the problem is\n- say what you would like the shop to do\nWrite at least 150 words. You do NOT need to write any addresses. Begin your letter as follows: Dear Sir or Madam,", "text": "Dear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to complain about a laptop I ordered from your website on 12 March (order number 58231), which arrived in an unusable condition.\n\nThe item was a 14-inch Asus Zenbook in silver, with 16 GB of memory, which I bought for 520 euros specifically because your listing described it as lightweight and suitable for travel. I need it for a work trip that begins at the end of this month.\n\nUnfortunately, when I opened the parcel, it was clear that it had been badly packed. There was no protective padding inside the box, and the laptop's lid was dented in one corner. More seriously, the screen has a crack running across it, and although the computer switches on, the display flickers constantly, making it impossible to use.\n\nGiven the circumstances, I would be grateful if you could send a replacement by express delivery, at no extra cost, so that it reaches me before I travel. If this is not possible, I would ask for a full refund, including the delivery charge, and for a courier to collect the damaged laptop.\n\nI look forward to your prompt reply.\n\nYours faithfully,\nDaniyar Seitkali", "overall_band": 7.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 8, "coherence_cohesion": 7, "lexical_resource": 8, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 7}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1g-visit-b5.5", "task_type": "task1_general", "prompt": "A friend from another country is planning to visit your city for a week. Write a letter to your friend. In your letter:\n- suggest where they could stay\n- recommend some things to do\n- tell them what they should bring\nWrite at least 150 words. You do NOT need to write any addresses. Begin your letter as follows: Dear ...,", "text": "Dear Sarah,\n\nI am so happy that you will come to Almaty next month! I want to give you some advices for your trip.\n\nAbout the place to stay, I think you should stay in a hotel in the city centre, because it is near to everything. There is a nice hotel near the Panfilov park, it is not very expensive and the rooms is clean. Of course you can also stay in my flat but it is small and far from centre.\n\nThere are many things to do in Almaty. You must visit the mountains, for example Medeu and Shymbulak, the views are amazing and you can take a cable car. Also you should try our national food like beshbarmak and baursak, I will take you to my favourite restaurant. In the evening we can walk on Arbat street.\n\nAbout what to bring, the weather in the mountains is cold even in summer so bring a warm jacket and comfortable shoes. Also bring some cash because some small shops dont accept cards.\n\nI can't wait to see you!\n\nBest wishes,\nAliya", "overall_band": 5.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 6, "coherence_cohesion": 5, "lexical_resource": 6, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 5}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t1g-visit-b6.5", "task_type": "task1_general", "prompt": "A friend from another country is planning to visit your city for a week. Write a letter to your friend. In your letter:\n- suggest where they could stay\n- recommend some things to do\n- tell them what they should bring\nWrite at least 150 words. You do NOT need to write any addresses. Begin your letter as follows: Dear ...,", "text": "Dear Tom,\n\nIt was great to hear that you've finally booked your trip to Astana! Here are a few tips to help you make the most of your week.\n\nAs for accommodation, I'd recommend staying somewhere on the left bank, near the Baiterek tower. Most of the modern sights are within walking distance there, and there are plenty of apartments which you can rent for a reasonable price. I'd offer you my sofa, but my flat is being renovated at the moment, so it's quite chaotic.\n\nThere's lots to keep you busy. You should definitely go up the Baiterek for the view over the city, and the Khan Shatyr shopping centre is worth a visit, if only to see the giant tent-like building. If you're interested in history, the National Museum has an excellent collection of gold jewellery. On the weekend we could also take a day trip to Burabay, a beautiful lake area about three hours away.\n\nAs for what to pack, the weather here can change very quickly, so bring a warm jacket even though it's May, and don't forget sunglasses, as the wind can be very strong and dusty.\n\nLet me know your flight details and I'll meet you at the airport.\n\nAll the best,\nErmek", "overall_band": 6.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 7, "coherence_cohesion": 6, "lexical_resource": 7, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 6}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-cities-b6.0", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Cities are becoming increasingly crowded. Some people say the solution is to move businesses and residents to the countryside. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "In recent years, cities around the world have become more and more crowded, which causes problems such as traffic jam, pollution and expensive housing. Some people suggest that businesses and residents should move to the countryside. I partly agree with this idea.\n\nOn the one hand, moving some companies to rural areas could reduce pressure on big cities. If offices and factories are built in smaller towns, people would not need to travel to the city centre every day, so there will be less traffic and air pollution. Also, housing in the countryside is much cheaper, therefore families could buy bigger homes and have a better quality of life. For example, in my country the government moved the capital to a new city and this helped to develop a region which was not develop before.\n\nOn the other hand, this solution is not suitable for everyone. The countryside often lacks good hospitals, universities and entertainment, so many young people would not want to live there. Moreover, some businesses, for instance banks or technology companies, need to be close to their customers and to other companies, and moving them far away could make them less successful. It would also be very expensive to build new roads and internet connection in rural areas.\n\nIn conclusion, I believe that moving some businesses and people to the countryside can be a part of the solution, but it cannot solve the problem alone. Governments should also improve public transport and build more affordable housing in cities.", "overall_band": 6.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 6, "coherence_cohesion": 6, "lexical_resource": 6, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 6}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-cities-b8.5", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Cities are becoming increasingly crowded. Some people say the solution is to move businesses and residents to the countryside. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "As urban populations continue to swell, congestion, soaring rents and poor air quality have become defining features of city life. One proposed remedy is to relocate businesses and residents to the countryside. While I accept that decentralisation has a role to play, I disagree that it offers a genuine solution on its own.\n\nThe appeal of the idea is easy to understand. Dispersing employers would ease the daily commute that clogs city roads, and the lower cost of rural land could allow families to live in larger, more affordable homes. The shift to remote working during the pandemic showed that many office-based roles do not, in fact, require a physical presence in the city centre, and some regional towns have since experienced a modest revival as professionals have moved out.\n\nYet the limitations are considerable. Cities concentrate not only people but also the institutions that make modern life possible: specialist hospitals, universities, cultural venues and dense labour markets in which workers can change jobs without moving house. Recreating this infrastructure across the countryside would be prohibitively expensive, and without it relocated residents would simply find themselves isolated. There is also an environmental paradox: scattering the population across rural areas tends to increase car dependency and consume farmland and natural habitats, which may leave the country worse off overall.\n\nA more realistic approach is to make cities work better rather than to empty them. Investment in public transport, higher-density affordable housing and mixed-use neighbourhoods, where people can live near their workplaces, addresses the causes of overcrowding directly, while targeted support for regional centres can relieve pressure at the margins.\n\nIn short, moving people and businesses to the countryside may help in specific cases, but as a general strategy it is costly and self-defeating; well-planned cities are the better answer.", "overall_band": 8.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 9, "coherence_cohesion": 8, "lexical_resource": 9, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 8}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-languages-b5.5", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Some people think that children should begin learning a foreign language at primary school rather than at secondary school. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "Nowadays learning foreign languages is very popular and many parents want their children start to learn English or other language as early as possible. Some people think that primary school is the best time for this, in my opinion the advantages is more than disadvantages.\n\nFirst of all, young children can learn languages more easy than teenagers. Their brain is very flexible and they can remember new words quickly, also they can copy the pronunciation of the teacher very well. For example, my younger sister started English when she was seven and now her accent is much better than mine. Moreover, if children start early they have more years to practice, so when they finish school they can speak fluently and it is helpful for university and future career.\n\nHowever, there are some disadvantages. Primary school children already have many subjects, like maths and reading in their native language, so a foreign language can make them tired and stressed. Also, some schools in villages don't have good teachers of English, so the children can learn wrong pronunciation or grammar and it is difficult to change it later. In addition some parents think that the child can forget his own language.\n\nIn conclusion, although there is some problems like stress and lack of teachers, I believe that starting foreign language in primary school have more advantages, because children learn faster and have more time to practice. The government should train more teachers so all children can have this opportunity. I think this is the best way for our country.", "overall_band": 5.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 6, "coherence_cohesion": 5, "lexical_resource": 6, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 5}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-languages-b7.5", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Some people think that children should begin learning a foreign language at primary school rather than at secondary school. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "Across the world, governments are debating how early children should begin studying a second language. I believe that introducing foreign languages at primary level brings benefits that clearly outweigh the drawbacks, provided that schools are properly resourced.\n\nThe strongest argument for an early start concerns how young children acquire language. Pupils of six or seven tend to absorb pronunciation and intonation almost effortlessly, largely because they are less self-conscious than adolescents and more willing to imitate. An early start also simply provides more time: a child who begins at seven will have had around ten years of exposure by the time they leave school, compared with five or six for someone who begins at twelve. In an economy where employers increasingly expect bilingual candidates, this head start can be decisive.\n\nThere are, admittedly, some genuine concerns. The primary curriculum is already crowded, and adding another subject may reduce the time available for literacy and numeracy, which remain the foundation of all later learning. A more practical problem is staffing. In rural areas especially, there may be few teachers who speak the language confidently, and a poor model at this stage can fix errors that are hard to undo later.\n\nHowever, these problems are matters of implementation rather than reasons to abandon the idea. Short, frequent sessions built around songs and games need not displace core subjects, and investment in teacher training, or even well-designed online materials, can compensate for staff shortages.\n\nOverall, while an early start places demands on schools, the advantages for children's fluency and future prospects are considerable, so I believe primary schools should introduce foreign languages as long as they receive the support they need.", "overall_band": 7.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 8, "coherence_cohesion": 7, "lexical_resource": 8, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 7}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-social-media-b4.5", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Many people believe that social media has had a negative effect on young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "Today social media is every where and young people use it all the time. Some people think it have negative effect on young people. I am agree with this opinion.\n\nsocial media is bad for young people because they spend to much time on it. They look instagram and tiktok all day and they dont do homework and they dont sleep. my friend he use phone until 3 in the night and in the morning he is very tired in the lesson. Also they dont talk with family, they only look the phone.\n\nSocial media also is bad because there is many fake news and bad people. young people believe every thing what they see and this is dangerous. Some people make bullying on internet and the children feel very sad and they have depression. Also they compare them self with the bloggers who have beautiful life and they think there life is bad.\n\nBut social media have also good side, you can talk with friends and find information for study. But i think bad side is more big then good side.\n\nIn conclusion social media is very bad for young people because they spend to much time and there is fake news and bullying. Parents must control there children and dont give them phone every time.", "overall_band": 4.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 5, "coherence_cohesion": 4, "lexical_resource": 5, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 4}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-social-media-b6.5", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Many people believe that social media has had a negative effect on young people. To what extent do you agree or disagree? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "Social media platforms such as Instagram and TikTok have become a central part of teenagers' lives. While these platforms offer some benefits, I largely agree that their overall effect on young people has been negative.\n\nThe most serious problem is the impact on mental health. Teenagers constantly compare themselves with influencers who present an edited, unrealistic version of their lives, and this often leads to low self-esteem and anxiety. In addition, cyberbullying has become more common, because it is easy for people to post cruel comments anonymously, and victims cannot escape it even when they are at home.\n\nAnother negative effect is on concentration and sleep. Many young people check their phones late at night, and the endless scrolling that these apps are designed to encourage means they get less rest than they need. As a result their performance at school can suffer. Teachers in my country often complains that students find it difficult to focus on a long text because they are used to short videos.\n\nOn the other hand, it would be unfair to say social media has no advantages. It allows young people to keep in touch with friends, to find communities who shares their interests and to learn about the world. During the pandemic, for example, it helped many students to feel less isolated.\n\nIn conclusion, although social media can connect people and provide information, I believe its effects on mental health, sleep and concentration make it more harmful than helpful for young people. Parents and schools should therefore teach teenagers to use it in a more balanced way.", "overall_band": 6.5, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 7, "coherence_cohesion": 6, "lexical_resource": 7, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 6}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-tuition-b4.0", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Some people believe that university students should pay the full cost of their studies, while others think that the government should pay for them. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Write at least 250 words.", "text": "Nowadays, many people think university is very expensive. Some people say students must pay all money and other people say goverment must pay. I will discuss both side.\n\nFirst, students should pay because they get the good job after university. When they have the degree they earn a lot of money so it is fair they pay. Also if student pay, they will study hard becouse the money is them own money.\n\nBut other people think goverment should pay. Education is very important for the country. If the goverment pay, more people can go to university and the country will be develop. Poor family can not pay the fees and this is not fair for they. My brother he want to study medicine but it cost too much money.\n\nIn my opinion goverment should pay because education is important for everybody and everybody need the education. In conclusion, both side have good reasons but i think goverment should pay.", "overall_band": 4.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 4, "coherence_cohesion": 4, "lexical_resource": 4, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 4}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-tuition-b7.0", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "Some people believe that university students should pay the full cost of their studies, while others think that the government should pay for them. Discuss both views and give your own opinion. Write at least 250 words.", "text": "Whether higher education should be funded by students themselves or by the state is a question that divides opinion in many countries. While there are reasonable arguments for making graduates bear the cost, I believe that governments should cover most of it.\n\nThose who favour student-funded education argue that a degree is primarily a personal investment. Graduates typically earn considerably more over their lifetimes than people without qualifications, so it seems fair that they, rather than taxpayers who may never have attended university, should pay. Supporters also claim that students who pay fees are more motivated and choose their courses more carefully, since wasting a year has a real financial cost.\n\nOn the other hand, there are strong reasons for public funding. Firstly, society as a whole benefits from an educated workforce; doctors, engineers and teachers trained at universities provide services that everyone relies on. Secondly, high fees discourage talented young people from poorer families, which means that access to university depends on wealth rather than ability. In my own country, many capable school leavers choose not to apply because they are afraid of long-term debt.\n\nIn my view, the second argument is more convincing. Although graduates do gain personally, the wider benefits to the economy and to social mobility are too important to leave to individual budgets. A reasonable compromise might be for the state to pay tuition while students cover their living costs, perhaps through part-time work or low-interest loans.\n\nIn conclusion, although asking students to pay the full cost may seem fair at first, I believe that government funding is the better approach because it rewards ability instead of wealth and benefits the whole society.", "overall_band": 7.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 7, "coherence_cohesion": 7, "lexical_resource": 7, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 7}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-work-family-b5.0", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "In many countries, people are spending more time at work and less time with their families. What are the causes of this? What can be done to solve this problem? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "In these days, many people work more hours and they spend less time with there family. There are some reasons for this problem and also there are some solutions.\n\nThe first reason is money. Life is very expensive now, for example the houses and food is more expensive than before, so people need to work more to pay for everything. Another reason is the companies. Many companies want workers to stay late in the office and if they don't stay they can lose there job. Also, the technology make people work at home, because they answer emails in the evening and in the weekend.\n\nThere are some solutions for this problem. Firstly, the government can make a law that people can not work more than 40 hours in one week. This will help people because they will have more free time. Secondly, companies should give workers more holidays and flexible time, so parents can take the children from school. Finally, people should turn off there phone after work and spend time with family, for example they can eat dinner together or go to the park. Families are very important for children and also for the parents.\n\nTo sum up, people work more because of money, companies and technology. I think the government and companies should do more to help people, and people also should change there habits. If we do this, families will be more happy and the society will be better.", "overall_band": 5.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 5, "coherence_cohesion": 5, "lexical_resource": 5, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 5}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}, {"id": "t2-work-family-b8.0", "task_type": "task2", "prompt": "In many countries, people are spending more time at work and less time with their families. What are the causes of this? What can be done to solve this problem? Write at least 250 words.", "text": "In many parts of the world, working hours have crept steadily upwards, and the time people spend with their families has shrunk accordingly. This trend has economic and technological roots, but it can be reversed if both employers and individuals take deliberate action.\n\nThe most obvious cause is financial pressure. In cities where housing costs have risen far faster than wages, many households need two full-time incomes, and often overtime as well, simply to cover rent or a mortgage. A second, less visible, cause is the way digital technology has blurred the boundary between the office and the home. Because employees can be reached by email or messaging apps at any hour, work increasingly spills into evenings and weekends, even for people whose contracts specify a standard working day. Finally, in competitive industries there is a culture of presenteeism, in which staying late is treated as evidence of commitment, regardless of whether it improves productivity.\n\nAddressing these causes requires action at several levels. Governments could follow the example of countries such as France, which have introduced a legal 'right to disconnect', allowing employees to ignore work messages outside their contracted hours. Employers, for their part, could judge staff on the results they achieve rather than the hours they log, and offer flexible schedules so that parents can, for instance, collect their children from school. At an individual level, families might agree on protected time, such as shared meals without phones, which is a modest step but a surprisingly effective one.\n\nIn conclusion, longer working hours stem mainly from economic pressure, constant connectivity and workplace culture. While no single measure will solve the problem, a combination of legal protection, smarter management and personal discipline could give families back the time they have lost.", "overall_band": 8.0, "criteria": {"task_achievement_response": 8, "coherence_cohesion": 8, "lexical_resource": 8, "grammatical_range_accuracy": 8}, "provenance_kind": "author_synthetic", "provenance": "synthetic; written for BandCraft AI; bands estimated by the author against the public IELTS band descriptors; not examiner-verified"}]$gold$::jsonb)
